  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>The Carlton Foundry is in the directories in <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref> from 1859 to 1890. Joseph Manchester has it at No. 41 in 1859. A. K. Smith is listed there from 1860 to 1880: at No. 41 to 1861, No. 56 from 1862 to 1873, No. 54 in 1874 and Nos 54–56 from 1875. From 1871 the listing is "civil and consulting engineer, and foundry", and in 1879 and 1880 it adds M.L.A. In 1873 it gives his private residence as 114 Leicester Street, where the directories list Alexander Kennedy Smith from 1867.</p>
        <p>Mephan Ferguson has the Carlton Foundry in 1885 and 1890, at Nos 114–128 in 1890. <ref target="civic?id=The%20Engineers%20of%20University%20Square">The Engineers of University Square</ref> says that Ferguson bought the foundry from Smith's estate.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>The <ref target="civic?id=Carlton%20Flour%20Mills">Carlton Flour Mills</ref> are next door for most of the foundry's life, at No. 39 and then No. 52, under Samuel and then George Ramsden (1859 to 1880). On the other side, the directories list Charles Musker, wheelwright (1859), Andrew Metherell, then Escott Metherell, carriers (1860 to 1863), M. J. Lopes, bottle merchant (1864), and McKenzie &amp; Co., moulders (1871 to 1873), with David McKenzie, iron founder, at No. 56 in 1874. The <ref target="civic?id=Rose%20Iron%20Works">Rose Iron Works</ref> are at No. 60 in 1876. In 1890 the Yarra Falls Roller Flour Mill Co. is listed beside the foundry.</p>
      </div>
